ARTICLES = [
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ BUYING GUIDES
dict(slug="how-deep-should-a-sofa-be", title="How Deep Should a Sofa Be?", cat="Buying Guides", mins=4,
     dek="Depth is the sofa dimension most people overlook online and notice first in person. Here is how to read it.",
     desc="A practical guide to sofa seat depth and overall depth, and how to choose the right one for how you actually sit.",
     img="journal-sofa-depth.jpg", tone="clay", mark="sofa",
     cta=("Deciding between two sofas?", "Send us the product links and your room dimensions. We will compare depth, comfort, materials, and value side by side."),
     body="""
<p>Two numbers matter, and retailers do not always make it obvious which one they are listing. <strong>Overall depth</strong> is the measurement from the front of the seat cushion to the back of the frame. <strong>Seat depth</strong> is the usable part: from the front edge of the cushion to the face of the back cushion. A sofa can have a 40-inch overall depth and a 22-inch seat depth, or the same overall depth and a 26-inch seat depth, and those two sofas will feel completely different.</p>
<h2>Seat depth and how you sit</h2>
<p>For most adults, a seat depth around 21 to 23 inches supports an upright posture with feet flat on the floor. Between 23 and 26 inches feels relaxed and works well for people who tuck their legs up or lean into the back. Beyond about 26 inches, the sofa is really designed for lounging: comfortable for stretching out, less comfortable for sitting up to talk or work. Neither end is wrong, but it helps to be honest about which one describes your living room most evenings.</p>
<p>If you already own a sofa you like, measure its seat depth before shopping. That number is a far more reliable guide than any product description.</p>
<h2>Overall depth and the room</h2>
<p>Overall depth is a floor-plan question. A deeper sofa pushes further into the room, and the walkway in front of it is where you will feel that. Aim to keep roughly 30 to 36 inches clear along the main path through the space, and 14 to 18 inches between the sofa front and the coffee table. In a narrow room, a 36- to 38-inch overall depth often makes the difference between a room that circulates and one that does not.</p>
<h2>Cushion fill changes the answer</h2>
<p>Listed seat depth assumes the cushion holds its shape. Soft, feather-wrapped cushions compress as you sit, which effectively deepens the seat by an inch or two; firm foam holds the listed dimension more closely. If a sofa is at the top of your comfortable range on paper, a softer fill may push it past it.</p>
<h2>A simple way to decide</h2>
<ul>
<li>Measure the seat depth of a sofa you find comfortable and note whether you sit upright or lounge on it.</li>
<li>Check the walkway you will have left after subtracting the sofa's overall depth from the room.</li>
<li>Compare listed seat depth against your reference number, adjusting for cushion fill.</li>
<li>Confirm the piece will fit through doorways and stairwells on the delivery path.</li>
</ul>
<p>Depth is the dimension that determines whether a sofa is lived on or merely sat on. Getting it right is usually worth more than any choice of fabric or leg style.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="how-to-choose-the-right-dining-table-size", title="How to Choose the Right Dining Table Size", cat="Buying Guides", mins=5,
     dek="Start with the room and the chairs, not the table. The right size falls out of a few simple clearances.",
     desc="How to size a dining table for your room: clearances, seats per person, round vs. rectangular, and when a smaller table is the better choice.",
     img="ec-solid-wood-table.jpg", tone="oat", mark="table",
     cta=("Not sure which table fits?", "Send us your dining room dimensions and how many people you usually seat. We will suggest sizes and shapes that work."),
     body="""
<p>Most dining tables are bought a size too large. The table looks fine in the showroom and in the product photo, then the chairs go in, someone tries to walk behind a seated guest, and the room stops working. The fix is to size from the outside in.</p>
<h2>Start with clearance, not the tabletop</h2>
<p>A chair needs about 18 to 20 inches to pull out and let someone sit. A person walking behind a seated guest needs roughly another 16 to 18 inches. Put together, plan on <strong>36 inches from the table edge to any wall or piece of furniture</strong> on sides where people need to pass, and 42 inches if that side is a main path through the house. On a side that is only ever for sitting, 30 to 32 inches is workable.</p>
<p>Subtract those clearances from the room's dimensions and you have the maximum table footprint. In a 10-by-12-foot room with a walkway on the long sides, that leaves a table roughly 48 inches wide and no longer than about 72 inches.</p>
<h2>Seats per person</h2>
<p>Give each diner about 24 inches of table edge for comfort, or 22 inches if the chairs are armless and you are fine with a closer table. A 72-inch rectangular table seats six comfortably (three per side) and eight only if the chairs are narrow and the end seats are used. A 36-inch-wide table is the practical minimum for serving dishes down the middle; 38 to 42 inches is more generous.</p>
<h2>Round, rectangular, or oval</h2>
<p>Round tables are the most forgiving in small square rooms because there are no corners to walk around, and everyone can see each other. A 42-inch round seats four; 48 inches seats four to five; 54 to 60 inches seats six. Beyond 60 inches, a round table becomes hard to reach across and rectangular or oval usually makes more sense. Oval tables give the seating of a rectangle with the softer circulation of a round; they are underused in rooms that are just slightly too tight for a rectangle.</p>
<h2>Pedestal or legs</h2>
<p>A pedestal base frees up the corners and lets you squeeze in an extra chair; four legs at the corners limit seating to the space between them. If you plan to seat the maximum, look at where the legs land relative to the chairs before ordering.</p>
<h2>When smaller is better</h2>
<p>An extension table is often the right answer for a room that hosts occasionally: a 60-inch table with one leaf covers most weeks and stretches to 78 inches for holidays. It costs less in daily circulation than a permanently large table and is usually cheaper than buying big and regretting it.</p>
<h2>Quick checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>Measure the room and subtract 36 inches on every side with foot traffic.</li>
<li>Allow 24 inches of edge per person.</li>
<li>Consider round or oval for square or tight rooms.</li>
<li>Check chair arm height against the apron height of the table; arms that do not tuck under waste space.</li>
<li>Tape the footprint on the floor before you buy.</li>
</ul>
"""),

dict(slug="sectional-vs-sofa-and-chairs", title="Sectional vs. Sofa + Chairs", cat="Buying Guides", mins=5,
     dek="One big piece or several smaller ones? The answer depends on how the room is used more than on how big it is.",
     desc="Sectional or sofa and chairs: how seating count, room shape, conversation, and flexibility should drive the choice.",
     img="ec-sectional.jpg", tone="sand", mark="sofa",
     cta=("Weighing a sectional against a sofa?", "Share your room dimensions and how you use the space. We will lay out both options to scale."),
     body="""
<p>This is the most common living room decision we are asked about, and the least well served by product photos. Both arrangements can seat the same number of people. What differs is how the room feels, how it circulates, and how easily it changes later.</p>
<h2>What a sectional does well</h2>
<p>A sectional turns seating into a single continuous surface. That is its strength for lounging, for families who pile on, and for rooms where the sofa faces a screen. It also anchors a large or open-plan room in a way that separate pieces struggle to, because the L or U shape defines an edge of the seating area without needing a wall.</p>
<p>The cost is flexibility. A sectional has one right position, its corner occupies floor space that is rarely sat in, and the chaise or return blocks a path on one side. In a room where people move through rather than around, that blocked side matters.</p>
<h2>What a sofa and chairs do well</h2>
<p>Separate pieces make conversation easier because people face each other rather than sitting in a row. They let light and sightlines pass through the room, which makes a smaller space feel bigger than a sectional of the same seat count would. And they can be rearranged: a chair moves to a bedroom, a new sofa slots in, the room is refreshed without starting over.</p>
<p>The trade-off is comfort for lounging and a more formal feel. Two chairs and a sofa also need a rug and coffee table doing more work to read as one group.</p>
<h2>How to decide</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Screen or people?</strong> If evenings are mostly facing a television, a sectional wins. If the room is for conversation and hosting, sofa and chairs.</li>
<li><strong>Which way does traffic go?</strong> Draw the paths through the room. If one crosses where a chaise would sit, that is your answer.</li>
<li><strong>How many seats do you actually need?</strong> Count real evenings, not holidays. A 3-seat sofa plus two chairs seats five and looks lighter than a five-seat sectional.</li>
<li><strong>How long will this room stay this room?</strong> If a move, a renovation, or a growing family is likely, separate pieces adapt; a sectional often does not.</li>
</ul>
<h2>The middle path</h2>
<p>A compact two-piece sectional (a sofa with a single chaise) plus one accent chair is often the best of both: continuous lounging on one side, a facing seat for conversation, and a chaise that can be swapped to the other side if the room changes. It is worth looking at before committing to either extreme.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="how-large-should-your-living-room-rug-be", title="How Large Should Your Living Room Rug Be?", cat="Buying Guides", mins=4,
     dek="Almost always larger than you think. Here is the rule, the exceptions, and how to check before buying.",
     desc="Living room rug sizing: the front-legs rule, common sizes, and how to tape it out before you order.",
     img="ec-wool-rug.jpg", tone="sage", mark="rug",
     cta=("Not sure which size?", "Send a photo and the room dimensions and we will mark the rug outline on your floor plan."),
     body="""
<p>The most common rug mistake is a 5-by-8 floating in the middle of a room designed for an 8-by-10 or 9-by-12. A too-small rug makes the seating look like it is drifting; the right size ties the group together and makes the room feel bigger, not smaller.</p>
<h2>The rule</h2>
<p>All the front legs of the seating should sit on the rug, with the rug extending 6 to 8 inches past the sides of the sofa. If the room allows, all four legs of every piece on the rug is the most finished look. A rug that stops short of the sofa front, so nothing touches it, is the arrangement to avoid.</p>
<h2>Working sizes</h2>
<ul>
<li>A typical 84- to 90-inch sofa with two chairs: <strong>8 by 10 feet</strong> is the usual answer.</li>
<li>A sectional or a larger seating group: <strong>9 by 12</strong>, sometimes 10 by 14.</li>
<li>An apartment sofa with one chair: <strong>6 by 9</strong> can work if the front legs land on it.</li>
<li>5 by 8 is generally too small for a seating group; it belongs under a coffee table alone or in an entry.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Leave floor at the edges</h2>
<p>Aim to leave 12 to 18 inches of bare floor between the rug and the walls. In a small room, 8 inches is acceptable. A rug that runs almost to the walls reads as carpet; a rug with a generous border reads as an intentional island.</p>
<h2>Check it before buying</h2>
<p>Use painter's tape to outline the candidate size on the floor, then step back and look from the doorway. This ten-minute test catches nearly every sizing error. It also shows you where the rug edge lands relative to doors, vents, and the path through the room.</p>
<h2>Pile and material, briefly</h2>
<p>For a living room with real traffic, a low to medium pile in wool or a wool blend is the durable, easy-to-clean choice and holds its look for years. High-pile and viscose rugs are lovely to look at and difficult to live with under a coffee table and feet. Whatever the material, a rug pad keeps it flat, protects the floor, and makes a thinner rug feel more substantial.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="what-to-measure-before-ordering-furniture", title="What to Measure Before Ordering Furniture", cat="Buying Guides", mins=5,
     dek="The piece fits the room. Does it fit through the door? A short checklist that prevents the most expensive mistake in furniture buying.",
     desc="A pre-order measuring checklist: room, delivery path, doorways, stairs, elevators, and the piece itself.",
     img="about-scale.jpg", tone="stone", mark="room",
     cta=("Want a second set of eyes?", "Send us the product page and photos of your entry and stairs. We will check the delivery path before you order."),
     body="""
<p>Returned furniture is usually returned for one reason: it did not fit. Not the room, which most people measure, but the route to the room, which most people do not. Ten minutes with a tape measure prevents nearly all of it.</p>
<h2>Measure the room</h2>
<p>Note the length and width of the wall the piece will sit against and the walkway that must remain in front of it. Record the height of window sills and outlets if the piece will sit under them; a sofa back that rises above a sill looks wrong from outside and blocks light.</p>
<h2>Measure the path</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Front door:</strong> width and height of the clear opening with the door open, not the frame.</li>
<li><strong>Hallways:</strong> width, plus the width of any turn. A 36-inch hall with a 90-degree turn will not pass a 40-inch-deep sofa carried upright.</li>
<li><strong>Interior doors:</strong> the narrowest one on the route.</li>
<li><strong>Stairs:</strong> width, ceiling height above the stairs, and the size of any landing where the piece must pivot.</li>
<li><strong>Elevators:</strong> door width, cab depth, and cab height. Many sofas go in diagonally; measure the diagonal.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Measure the piece</h2>
<p>Product pages list width, depth, and height. For delivery you also want the <strong>diagonal depth</strong>: the distance from the top of the back to the front of the arm, measured corner to corner. Most manufacturers provide it on request and some publish it. As a rule, a sofa fits through a doorway if either its height or its diagonal depth is less than the opening width. Remove the legs if they unscrew; it often buys the crucial two inches.</p>
<h2>Tape it on the floor</h2>
<p>Outline the footprint on the floor with painter's tape and live with it for a day. Walk around it. Open nearby doors and drawers. This shows you what a floor plan cannot: whether the coffee table still leaves a path, whether a door swing clips the chair, whether the piece is simply too much for the room.</p>
<h2>Before you click order</h2>
<ul>
<li>Confirm the delivery method. Curbside means it stops at the curb.</li>
<li>Check whether the piece ships assembled, in modules, or flat. Modular sectionals solve most path problems.</li>
<li>Read the return policy for oversized items. Restocking fees on furniture are common and large.</li>
<li>Photograph the entry and stairs; if there is any doubt, send them to the retailer or to us before ordering.</li>
</ul>
"""),

dict(slug="performance-fabric-what-actually-matters", title="Performance Fabric: What Actually Matters?", cat="Buying Guides", mins=5,
     dek="The label promises a lot. Here is what the terms mean, which ones matter for your household, and where the trade-offs are.",
     desc="A plain-language guide to performance fabrics: rub counts, stain resistance, cleaning codes, fiber types, and what to actually ask for.",
     img="post-sectional-2.jpg", tone="clay", mark="sofa",
     cta=("Comparing fabrics on two sofas?", "Send us the fabric names and we will compare fiber, cleanability, and durability side by side."),
     body="""
<p>"Performance fabric" is a marketing term, not a standard. It usually means a textile engineered to resist stains, hold up to abrasion, and clean with water. Some do all three very well. Some barely differ from ordinary upholstery. The details tell you which.</p>
<h2>Abrasion: the rub count</h2>
<p>Durability is tested by rubbing the fabric until it wears through, reported as double rubs (Wyzenbeek, common in the US) or cycles (Martindale, common in Europe). For a family sofa, look for at least 30,000 double rubs or 40,000 Martindale cycles; contract-grade fabrics run higher. Beyond that range the numbers stop being meaningful for a home, so do not pay for 100,000 double rubs unless you have another reason to.</p>
<h2>Stain resistance: fiber vs. finish</h2>
<p>There are two ways a fabric resists stains, and the difference matters over years. <strong>Inherently stain-resistant fibers</strong> such as solution-dyed acrylic or olefin resist stains because of what they are made of; the resistance does not wash out. <strong>Topical finishes</strong> applied to cotton, linen, or polyester resist stains until the coating wears off, typically after a few years of use and cleaning. Both work when new. Only the first still works after five years.</p>
<h2>The cleaning code</h2>
<p>Every upholstery fabric carries a cleaning code, and it is the most practical detail on the spec sheet. <strong>W</strong> means water-based cleaning; <strong>S</strong> means solvent only; <strong>W/S</strong> either; <strong>X</strong> vacuum only. For a household with children or pets, W or W/S is the one to insist on. A fabric marketed as performance with an S code is a red flag.</p>
<h2>What performance fabrics give up</h2>
<p>Most are synthetic or synthetic blends. Compared with linen or wool they can feel slightly flatter, show less texture, and pill more under heavy use, particularly looser weaves. The best ones now come close to natural fibers in hand, but it is worth touching a swatch rather than trusting a photo. Order swatches from any brand you are considering; nearly all offer them free.</p>
<h2>What to ask for</h2>
<ul>
<li>Cleaning code W or W/S.</li>
<li>At least 30,000 double rubs or 40,000 Martindale.</li>
<li>Whether stain resistance is inherent to the fiber or a topical treatment.</li>
<li>A swatch, tested at home with whatever your household actually spills.</li>
<li>Whether cushion covers are removable; it matters more than any fabric property.</li>
</ul>
"""),

dict(slug="choosing-outdoor-furniture-that-lasts", title="Choosing Outdoor Furniture That Lasts", cat="Buying Guides", mins=5,
     dek="Sun, rain, and neglect are the real tests. Materials that pass them, and the ones that only look like they will.",
     desc="How to choose outdoor furniture by frame material, cushion construction, and climate, plus the maintenance each option really needs.",
     img="lookbook-05-wide.jpg", tone="sage", mark="sofa",
     cta=("Planning a patio or balcony?", "Tell us the space, the climate, and how much upkeep you are willing to do. We will source pieces that fit."),
     body="""
<p>Outdoor furniture fails in predictable ways: frames rust or rot, cushions hold water and mildew, finishes chalk in the sun. Choosing well is mostly a matter of matching materials to your climate and to how much maintenance you will honestly do.</p>
<h2>Frames</h2>
<p><strong>Teak</strong> is the benchmark: dense, oily, and stable outdoors for decades. Left alone it weathers to silver-grey; oiled once or twice a year it stays honey-toned. Expect to pay for it. <strong>Powder-coated aluminum</strong> is the practical modern choice: it does not rust, is light enough to move, and needs almost nothing. Look for a thick powder coat and welded rather than bolted joints. <strong>Steel</strong> is heavier and stronger but will rust wherever the coating chips, which in a wet climate is a matter of time. <strong>Synthetic wicker</strong> over aluminum is durable if the weave is HDPE rather than PVC; cheap PVC wicker cracks after a couple of summers. <strong>Acacia and eucalyptus</strong> are budget hardwoods that look like teak new and need sealing every year to stay that way.</p>
<h2>Cushions</h2>
<p>The fabric matters less than the foam. <strong>Quick-dry reticulated foam</strong> lets water pass straight through and dries in hours; standard foam holds water for days and grows mildew from the inside. For the cover, solution-dyed acrylic (the Sunbrella family and similar) resists fading and mildew far better than polyester, which fades within a season in strong sun. Zippered, removable covers are worth insisting on.</p>
<h2>Match the climate</h2>
<p>In the wet Pacific Northwest, drainage and dry time matter most: aluminum or teak frames, quick-dry foam, covers or storage for the off-season. In hot, dry climates, fade resistance and heat come first: light colors, acrylic fabrics, and awareness that dark metal in full sun becomes untouchable. Coastal air corrodes steel quickly and pits untreated aluminum; marine-grade finishes are worth the premium there.</p>
<h2>Scale and layout</h2>
<p>Outdoor pieces are often deeper and lower than indoor equivalents, and small balconies punish that. Measure the space and keep a 30-inch path to the door. On a balcony, a bistro table with two folding chairs usually serves better than a lounge set that leaves no room to stand.</p>
<h2>Honest maintenance</h2>
<ul>
<li>Teak: oil yearly or accept grey. Aluminum: rinse. Steel: touch up chips immediately.</li>
<li>Cushions: stand them on edge after rain; store or cover for winter.</li>
<li>Covers help but trap moisture if the furniture goes under them wet.</li>
</ul>
<p>The set that lasts is usually the one whose upkeep you will actually do, not the one with the longest warranty.</p>
"""),

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ PRODUCT COMPARISONS
dict(slug="dining-tables-solid-wood-vs-veneer", title="Dining Tables: Solid Wood vs. Veneer", cat="Product Comparisons", mins=4,
     dek="Veneer is not a lesser version of solid wood. It is a different construction with different strengths, and the better choice more often than people expect.",
     desc="Solid wood vs. veneer dining tables: stability, repairability, cost, appearance, and how to tell quality veneer from cheap.",
     img="lookbook-04-detail-a.jpg", tone="oat", mark="table",
     cta=("Comparing two tables?", "Send us the links. We will check construction, finish, and dimensions and tell you what the listings leave out."),
     body="""
<p>Solid wood has the better reputation; veneer has the worse one, mostly because the word gets used for everything from fine furniture to laminated particleboard. Understanding what each actually is makes the choice much simpler.</p>
<h2>What they are</h2>
<p>A <strong>solid wood</strong> table top is planks of lumber glued edge to edge. A <strong>veneer</strong> top is a thin slice of real wood, typically under a millimeter, bonded to a stable core. The core is what varies: plywood or engineered wood in good furniture, particleboard in cheap furniture. The veneer itself is real wood either way; the core decides quality.</p>
<h2>Where solid wood wins</h2>
<p>Repairability. A solid top can be sanded and refinished many times, so scratches, water rings, and decades of use are recoverable. It also has an edge grain and a weight that veneer imitates but does not match. For a table you expect to keep for thirty years and pass on, solid wood is the sound choice.</p>
<h2>Where veneer wins</h2>
<p>Stability. Solid wood moves with humidity; wide tops can cup, and joints can open in a dry winter. Veneer over plywood barely moves, which is why large tops, book-matched grain, and complex patterns are almost always veneer. It is also lighter, usually less expensive for the same look, and lets a maker use rare or figured wood without the cost of solid stock. Many of the most respected mid-century designs are veneered for exactly these reasons.</p>
<h2>What to check on a veneer table</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Core material.</strong> Plywood or a dense engineered core is good; particleboard is not. Weight is a clue.</li>
<li><strong>Veneer thickness.</strong> Thicker veneer survives a light sanding; paper-thin veneer does not survive a deep scratch.</li>
<li><strong>Edges.</strong> Solid wood edge banding that matches the top is a sign of a well-made piece; plastic edge tape is the opposite.</li>
<li><strong>Finish.</strong> A catalyzed lacquer or conversion varnish protects veneer far better than a thin oil.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What to check on a solid table</h2>
<ul>
<li>Breadboard ends or a floating attachment that lets the top move seasonally.</li>
<li>Whether the top is one species throughout or a solid top on a different-wood base, which is fine but should be disclosed.</li>
<li>How the finish handles water. Oil finishes look beautiful and mark easily; hardwax oils are a good compromise.</li>
</ul>
<p>The short version: if you will refinish it someday, buy solid. If you want a large, stable, precisely finished top at a sensible price, a well-made veneer table is not a compromise.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="upholstery-options-for-homes-with-kids", title="Upholstery Options for Homes With Kids", cat="Product Comparisons", mins=4,
     dek="Fabric, leather, slipcovers, and the details around them that matter more than the material itself.",
     desc="Upholstery for families: performance fabrics, leather, slipcovers, cushion construction, and the design details that hide wear.",
     img="lookbook-02-detail-a.jpg", tone="stone", mark="sofa",
     cta=("Choosing a family sofa?", "Tell us about your household and we will shortlist pieces built to take it."),
     body="""
<p>Every sofa in a house with children becomes a fort, a snack table, and occasionally a trampoline. The question is not how to prevent that but how to choose a piece that looks fine afterward.</p>
<h2>Performance fabric</h2>
<p>The default answer, and usually the right one. Look for a W or W/S cleaning code, a fiber with inherent rather than sprayed-on stain resistance, and a rub count above 30,000. Mid-tone colors and subtle texture hide far more than a flat pale weave. Our guide to <a href="performance-fabric-what-actually-matters.html">what actually matters in performance fabric</a> covers the details.</p>
<h2>Leather</h2>
<p>Leather wipes clean, does not hold crumbs, and improves with age in a way fabric never does. The catch is which leather. <strong>Top-grain and full-grain</strong> with a protected (pigmented) finish are the family-proof kind; <strong>aniline</strong> leathers are gorgeous and absorb every spill; <strong>bonded leather</strong> is not really leather and peels within a few years. Leather scratches from pets and zippers, so it suits some households better than others.</p>
<h2>Slipcovers</h2>
<p>A washable slipcover is the only upholstery that resets fully. Modern slipcovered sofas fit far better than the baggy versions of twenty years ago, and a second cover in a different color is a cheap way to change the room. The trade-offs are a softer, less tailored look and the effort of removing and replacing a cover after washing.</p>
<h2>The details that matter more</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Removable cushion covers.</strong> If seat and back cushion covers zip off, most accidents become laundry rather than professional cleaning.</li>
<li><strong>Reversible cushions.</strong> Doubles the life of the seat.</li>
<li><strong>Tight-back vs. loose cushions.</strong> Tight backs have fewer crevices for crumbs; loose cushions are more comfortable and can be flipped.</li>
<li><strong>Frame and legs.</strong> A kiln-dried hardwood frame and a low, solid base survive climbing. Tall thin legs and light frames do not.</li>
<li><strong>Rounded edges.</strong> On coffee tables especially, but on sofa arms too.</li>
</ul>
<h2>A reasonable combination</h2>
<p>For most families: a performance fabric in a mid-tone, removable and reversible cushion covers, a low solid base, and a rug that is easier to clean than the sofa. Leather if pets are not scratchers. Slipcovers if you want to stop worrying entirely.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="best-furniture-materials-for-pet-owners", title="Best Furniture Materials for Pet Owners", cat="Product Comparisons", mins=4,
     dek="Claws, fur, and the occasional accident. Materials that handle them, and a few popular ones that do not.",
     desc="Furniture materials for homes with dogs and cats: tight-weave fabrics, leather, microfiber, wood finishes, and what to avoid.",
     img="ec-compact-sofa.jpg", tone="clay", mark="sofa",
     cta=("Furnishing with pets in mind?", "Tell us who lives in the house and we will narrow the field to pieces that will hold up."),
     body="""
<p>Pets test furniture in three ways: claws pull threads, fur clings to texture, and accidents soak in. A material that handles all three is rare, so it helps to know which problem is biggest in your house.</p>
<h2>For claws</h2>
<p>Loose weaves, bouclé, chenille, and anything with a visible loop are the fabrics cats pull apart. <strong>Tight, flat weaves</strong> in a synthetic or synthetic blend hold up best; <strong>microfiber</strong> and <strong>microsuede</strong> are particularly resistant because there is nothing for a claw to catch. Leather resists cat claws poorly and dog nails moderately; if you love leather, a heavily protected top-grain with a distressed finish hides marks better than a smooth one.</p>
<h2>For fur</h2>
<p>Fur clings to texture and shows on contrast. Smooth fabrics in a color close to the animal's coat make the problem nearly invisible; a black dog on a cream bouclé is the worst case. Leather and tightly woven synthetics release fur with a wipe or a vacuum pass. Velvet, oddly, is easier than it looks: short-pile synthetic velvets shed fur well and clean with water.</p>
<h2>For accidents</h2>
<p>Water-cleanable (code W) fabric with inherent stain resistance, and removable cushion covers. Leather wipes clean but can be stained by urine if not caught quickly. Avoid untreated linen and cotton, which absorb immediately, and any fabric with an S cleaning code.</p>
<h2>Wood and hard surfaces</h2>
<p>Dog nails scratch soft floors and soft-finished tables. Harder species (oak, maple, ash) and film finishes (lacquer, conversion varnish) mark less than pine or oil-finished walnut. Rounded edges save both the furniture and the pet.</p>
<h2>A practical shortlist</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Sofa:</strong> tight-weave performance fabric or microfiber, mid-tone, removable covers, low solid base.</li>
<li><strong>Rug:</strong> low-pile wool or a washable synthetic; avoid viscose and high pile entirely.</li>
<li><strong>Tables:</strong> hardwood with a durable film finish, or stone.</li>
<li><strong>Bedding and throws:</strong> washable, in the pet's color.</li>
</ul>
<p>And a throw over the favorite spot solves more than any material choice. It is not elegant; it works.</p>
"""),

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ DESIGN NOTES
dict(slug="how-to-mix-wood-tones", title="How to Mix Wood Tones", cat="Design Notes", mins=4,
     dek="Matching every wood in a room is not the goal. Here is how to mix them so the result looks collected rather than accidental.",
     desc="A practical approach to mixing wood tones: undertones, a dominant wood, repetition, and using rugs and textiles as buffers.",
     img="ec-sideboard.jpg", tone="oat", mark="storage",
     cta=("Not sure the new piece will work with what you have?", "Send us photos of the room and the product link. We will tell you if the tones sit together."),
     body="""
<p>Rooms where every wood matches look like a showroom. Rooms where woods are mixed with no plan look like a garage sale. The difference is a few simple decisions made before the second piece arrives.</p>
<h2>Find the undertone</h2>
<p>Every wood leans warm (red, orange, yellow), cool (grey, ashy), or neutral. Cherry and mahogany are warm; walnut is warm-neutral; white oak is neutral to slightly warm; ash and driftwood finishes are cool. Woods with the same undertone mix easily regardless of how light or dark they are. Woods with clashing undertones, an orange-toned oak next to a grey-washed pine, fight even when their values are similar. Pick an undertone family and stay mostly inside it.</p>
<h2>Choose a dominant wood</h2>
<p>Usually the floor, or the largest piece if the floor is carpet or tile. Everything else is secondary to it. Two or three woods in a room is comfortable; more than that needs deliberate repetition to hold together.</p>
<h2>Repeat each wood at least twice</h2>
<p>A single walnut chair in a room of oak looks like a mistake. The same chair with a walnut picture frame or a walnut bowl on the shelf looks intentional. Small repeats count: a tray, a lamp base, a frame.</p>
<h2>Contrast on purpose</h2>
<p>When two woods sit side by side, make the difference obvious. A medium oak table with medium-dark walnut chairs reads as an almost-match and looks off. The same table with clearly dark chairs, or clearly light ones, reads as a choice.</p>
<h2>Use buffers</h2>
<p>A rug between a wood floor and a wood table, or a fabric seat between a wood frame and a wood floor, gives the eye a rest and lets more woods coexist. Upholstered dining chairs are the easiest way to put a new wood table on an existing wood floor.</p>
<h2>Painted and metal pieces help</h2>
<p>A black metal lamp, a white painted cabinet, a stone top: each one reduces the number of woods competing and gives the mix room to breathe. If a room has too many woods, the fix is often not another wood at all.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="why-furniture-scale-matters-more-than-style", title="Why Furniture Scale Matters More Than Style", cat="Design Notes", mins=4,
     dek="A beautiful piece at the wrong size is a mistake. An ordinary piece at the right size is most of a good room.",
     desc="Why proportion and scale do more for a room than style, and how to check both before you buy.",
     img="lookbook-01-wide.jpg", tone="sand", mark="room",
     cta=("Want a scale check before you order?", "Send us the room dimensions and the pieces you are considering. We will draw them in to scale."),
     body="""
<p>When a room feels off and no one can say why, the cause is almost always scale. The sofa is a little too big, the coffee table a little too small, the lamp too short for the table it sits on. Style choices get the attention, but scale is what people feel.</p>
<h2>Scale is about relationships</h2>
<p>No piece is the right size on its own. A 96-inch sofa is right in a 16-foot room and wrong in a 12-foot room; a 42-inch coffee table is right in front of that sofa and lost in front of a sectional. When evaluating a piece, the question is never "is this big" but "is this big relative to the wall, the rug, the piece next to it, and the people who will use it."</p>
<h2>Some relationships that usually hold</h2>
<ul>
<li>A coffee table about two-thirds the length of the sofa, with its top within an inch or two of the seat height.</li>
<li>Side tables level with or slightly below the sofa arm.</li>
<li>A table lamp whose shade bottom sits around eye level when seated, roughly 24 to 30 inches above the table.</li>
<li>Art hung with its center about 57 to 60 inches from the floor, and above a sofa spanning roughly two-thirds of the sofa's width.</li>
<li>A rug large enough that the front legs of all seating sit on it.</li>
<li>A dining table with 36 inches of clearance on each side that people walk along.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Visual weight, not just dimensions</h2>
<p>Two sofas of identical size can feel very different. One with a low back, thin arms, and legs that show the floor reads light and suits a small room. One with a high back, thick arms, and a skirt reads heavy and needs space around it. In a tight room, choose pieces that show floor beneath them and keep sightlines open; the room will feel larger at the same footprint.</p>
<h2>Why this beats style</h2>
<p>Style is easy to change: a different fabric, a new lamp, a rug. Scale is baked into the piece and cannot be adjusted after delivery. A room of well-scaled ordinary pieces reads as calm and finished. A room of striking pieces at the wrong scale reads as busy no matter how good each piece is. Get the sizes right first; the style will have something to sit on.</p>
<h2>How to check</h2>
<p>Tape the footprint on the floor. Cut cardboard to the height of a sofa back or a headboard and stand it in place. Photograph the room from the doorway with the mock-up in it. These are unglamorous steps, and they are what professionals do before ordering.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="how-to-make-a-neutral-room-feel-warm", title="How to Make a Neutral Room Feel Warm", cat="Design Notes", mins=4,
     dek="Beige is not the problem. Flatness is. A few moves that give a neutral room depth without adding color.",
     desc="How to warm up a neutral room with texture, layered lighting, wood, contrast, and natural materials.",
     img="home-detail.jpg", tone="oat", mark="lamp",
     cta=("Room feels flat and you are not sure why?", "Send a few photos. We will suggest the two or three changes that will make the biggest difference."),
     body="""
<p>A neutral room that feels cold usually has plenty of the right colors and none of the right textures. Warmth comes from variation: in surface, in light, in the depth of one beige against another. Here is where to add it.</p>
<h2>Layer textures, not colors</h2>
<p>In a single-color scheme, texture does the work color would do elsewhere. Aim for at least five distinct surfaces in view: a nubby wool rug, a smooth linen sofa, a ribbed or bouclé chair, a wood table with visible grain, a ceramic or stone object. If everything is smooth, add something rough; if everything is matte, add one thing with sheen.</p>
<h2>Warm the light, and layer it</h2>
<p>One overhead fixture makes any room feel like an office. Add a floor lamp, a table lamp, and ideally a third source at a different height, all on warm bulbs (2700K is the safe choice). Shades in linen or paper glow; opaque shades throw light down and leave walls cold. If a single change would do the most, it is this one.</p>
<h2>Add real wood</h2>
<p>Wood is the most reliable warming material. A walnut side table, an oak stool, a wood picture frame. Mid to dark tones warm faster than pale ash; one or two pieces are enough.</p>
<h2>Introduce contrast</h2>
<p>All-light rooms wash out. A dark element, a charcoal lamp, a black metal frame, an espresso wood, gives the light tones something to be light against. Small doses are enough.</p>
<h2>Vary the neutrals</h2>
<p>Beige, cream, oat, greige, camel, and taupe are all neutral and all different. A room that uses three of them reads richer than one that uses a single shade throughout. Keep the undertones consistent (all warm or all cool) so they read as family rather than mismatch.</p>
<h2>Soften the edges</h2>
<p>Curtains to the floor, a throw over the arm, a rounded ottoman instead of a square table, a plant with real leaves. Hard-edged rooms feel cold in any color. Soft-edged rooms feel warm in any color.</p>
<p>None of this requires a bolder palette. It requires the neutral room to have as much variety in touch and light as a colorful room has in hue.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="choosing-lighting-for-an-open-concept-space", title="Choosing Lighting for an Open-Concept Space", cat="Design Notes", mins=5,
     dek="One big room, several jobs. Lighting is how an open plan reads as living, dining, and kitchen instead of a hall.",
     desc="Lighting an open-concept space: defining zones with fixtures, layering ambient, task, and accent light, and keeping finishes coherent.",
     img="ec-pendant.jpg", tone="stone", mark="lamp",
     cta=("Lighting an open plan?", "Send us the floor plan and ceiling heights. We will suggest a fixture scheme by zone."),
     body="""
<p>Open-concept spaces are usually lit as one room: a grid of recessed cans and not much else. The result is even, bright, and characterless. The better approach is to light each zone as if it were its own room, then make sure the pieces belong to the same family.</p>
<h2>Give each zone an anchor</h2>
<p>A fixture that says "this is the dining area" does more to define space than any rug. A pendant or a linear fixture centered over the table, hung so its bottom is 30 to 36 inches above the tabletop, marks the dining zone. A floor lamp beside the sofa and a table lamp on the console mark the living zone. Pendants over the island mark the kitchen. When each zone has a visible fixture, the room stops reading as a hall.</p>
<h2>Layer within each zone</h2>
<p>Three layers, in every zone where you can manage it. <strong>Ambient</strong> light is the general fill: recessed cans, a ceiling fixture, or bounced light from a floor lamp. <strong>Task</strong> light is for doing things: pendants over the island, a reading lamp by the chair. <strong>Accent</strong> light is for mood and depth: a picture light, a lamp on a shelf, a low table lamp on the sideboard. Rooms lit only with ambient light feel flat; rooms with all three feel finished.</p>
<h2>Put everything on dimmers</h2>
<p>In an open plan you will want the kitchen bright while the living area is dim, or the dining pendant low while everything else is off. Separate switches per zone and dimmers on all of them are the cheapest upgrade with the biggest effect. Warm bulbs (2700K) throughout; mixing color temperatures across an open room is visibly jarring.</p>
<h2>Keep the fixtures related, not identical</h2>
<p>The pendants over the island, the fixture over the table, and the lamps in the living area should share something: a finish, a material, a shape family. Black metal and linen shades; brass and white glass; oak and paper. They should not be a matched set from one product line, which looks like a catalog page, nor five unrelated statements, which looks like noise. Two finishes across the whole space is a good ceiling.</p>
<h2>Scale to the ceiling and the table</h2>
<p>A dining fixture should be roughly one-half to two-thirds the width of the table. Island pendants in a row should be spaced so their edges are at least 24 inches apart, with 6 inches or more from the island ends. For a ceiling under nine feet, choose fixtures with a compact vertical profile; for a tall or vaulted ceiling, let the fixture drop and take up real space, or it will disappear.</p>
<h2>Do not forget the walls</h2>
<p>Wall sconces flanking a fireplace or beside the sofa, and a picture light over art, pull light off the ceiling and onto vertical surfaces. Open plans have a lot of ceiling and often not enough happening on the walls; sconces fix both.</p>
"""),
]
